==English==

===Etymology===
From {{prefix|en|a|shore}}; see also ''[[onshore]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈʃɔː/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈʃɔɹ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈʃo(ː)ɹ/|a=rhotic,non-horse-hoarse}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈʃoə/|a=non-rhotic,non-horse-hoarse}}
* {{homophones|en|assure|aa=pour-poor}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ashore.ogg|a=US}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} On the [[land]] as opposed to [[onboard]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|II|ii|text=I shall no more to sea, to sea, / Here shall I die '''ashore'''—{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} On, or towards the [[shore]].
#: {{syn|en|aland|qq1=rare, poetic}}
#: {{ux|en|The canoe sailed '''ashore'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|II|ii|text=Swum '''ashore''', man, like a duck. I can swim like a duck, I'll be sworn.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:National Geographic|year=1989|month=May|volume=175|number=5|title=Braving the Northwest Passage|author=Jeff MacInnis|page=592
|text=Driven '''ashore''' by a violent storm that we barely survived, Mike and I camp (below) at Aston Bay on Somerset Island.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:San Francisco Chronicle|date=December 12 2012|title=Squid deaths are a many-tentacled mystery|author=Peter Fimrite
|text=Several hundred [squid] washed '''ashore''' over the weekend on beaches along a 12-mile stretch in Santa Cruz County, one of the largest local mass strandings that researchers can remember.
|url=https://www.sfchronicle.com/science/article/Squid-deaths-are-a-many-tentacled-mystery-4110394.php}}

====Usage notes====
* When a ship collides with the shore it is said to have ''run [[ashore]]''; when a ship collides with a submerged object, such as a reef or sandbar, it is said to have ''run [[aground]]''.

